{
  "abstract": "Leonard Carlitz’s Theorem 1 (1954) determines a signed Euler number modulo an odd prime power p^e when its index is a positive multiple of the totient of p^e. The residue is zero for p congruent to 1 modulo 4 and two for p congruent to 3 modulo 4. We formalize this theorem by a finite alternating-sum argument. For m = 2n−1 and n positive, the reduced numerator a(n) of the Euler polynomial E_m(n) is always divisible by m², and is divisible by m³ exactly when m divides the signed Euler number of index m−1. At odd primes m = p the latter holds exactly when p is congruent to 1 modulo 4. These results prove the conjectures recorded in OEIS A291897 and A292911.",
